Billie Goodson Pierce was born in Marianna, Florida 
on June 8, 1907, and raised in Pensacola. Her entire fam- 
ily was musical. Her six sisters, her cousins and her par- 
ents all sang and played piano. The first tune Billie picked 
out on the piano was a blues, she had had no lessons and 
was so young she could barely sing the words. “Most all 
my days I’ve been playing music. I started playing the 
blues. My mother and father, you know, were very re- 
ligious people. Me and my sisters would get around the 
piano and have a good time playing ragtime and singing 
the blues. Somebody watched out for daddy and when 
he’d come, we’d break into, “What A Friend We Have In 
Jesus.” He never knew the difference.” Many of the best 
New Orleans bands appeared in the halls and parks of 
Pensacola. Billie and her sisters would wait until their 
parents had gone to bed and then slip out to the dance. 
There she heard many of the bands she was later to play 
with; Buddy Petit, Kid Rena, Punch Miller, Papa Celes- 
tin, Red Allen and others. : 

Whenever the local theatres needed a substitute piano 
player they would send for one of the Goodson girls. 
When Billie was fifteen she took Clarence Williams’ place 
for a week as accompanist for the great blues singer 
Bessie Smith. Billie says, “A blues ain't but a blues, just a 
set of poems, it’s what you put in them that makes them 
different.” It was shortly after playing for Bessie Smith 
that Billie and her older sister Edna went on tour with 
the Mighty Wiggle Carnival. Both girls were in the 
chorus and Billie also played the organ for the minstrel 
show. From there Billie began a succession of jops with 
shows and orchestras that eventually brought her to New 
Orleans in 1929. Two of the better known groups she 
travelled with were the nine piece Nighthawk Orchestra 
from Alabama and Billie and Mary Mack’s Merry Makers 


_from New Orleans. 


In New Orleans her first job was to substitute for 
her sister Sadie on the Lake Pontchartrain excursion 
steamer Madison with Buddy Petit’s band. She was only in 
the city a year when the depression found her, along with 
dozens of other musicians, in the rowdy honky tonks 
along the Decatur Street waterfront. This street across 
from the French Market was lined with low dives that 
featured cheap whiskey and dancing to three or four 
piece bands. Billie worked at Popeye’s, Charlie Palooka’s, 
Corrine’s, and in 1935 was at the Kingfish on the corner 
of Ursuline and Decatur working for $1.00 a night. Here 
George Lewis was the clarinetist in her four piece band. 
This was the beginning of a friendship and musical asso- 
ciation that has lasted through the years. Burke Steven- 
son, their trumpeter was replaced by De De Pierce, who 
had been playing up the street in Sadie’s band at Mama's 
and Papa’s. Billie announced to her sister, “If De De 
isn’t your boy friend I could sure love the hell out of 
him.” Three weeks later, on March 28, 1935, at St. Peter 
Claver Church, ‘Billie and De De were married with 
George Lewis as their best man. 


