“De De” Joseph La Croix Pierce was born in New 
Orleans on February 18, 1904. As a small boy he was 
given a toy trumpet by his cousin who played in an all 
girl orchestra. He learned to play it by himself but later 
took lessons from Professor Chaligny, who was the leader 
of the Onward Band, and from Kid Rena. De De’s fam- 
ily were Creoles and usually spoke French in the house. 
It was here that De De learned most of his Creole folk 
songs such as Sallee Dame. This is a song that is sung 
by maskers on Mardi Gras day. The masker would take 
his guitar and walk through the street until he found 
someone whom he recognized and would greet them with 
the salutation “Sallee dame,” bow and give them a big 
smile and then make up several verses about them, al- 
ways trying to find something nasty to say about their 
personal life. 

De De’s father was a well established brick mason 
and wanted De De to follow in his trade, which he obe- 
diently did. But music was his first love and he always 
found time to play on the side. Because of his other job 
he very seldom went on the road but preferred to job 
around the city. He was always in demand for Brass 
Band jobs and frequently played between innings at Hei- 
nemann Park, all sorts of parties, and the Fair Grounds 
where he first- heard Louis Armstrong play Peanut Ven- 
dor, and where the idea for his version of Eh La Bas 


. first came to him. 


After Billie and De De were married they seldom 
worked a job unless. they were playing together, except 
for De De’s Brass Band jobs and Billie taking a job for 
several months with A. J. Piron at the Absinthe House. 
De De began spending much more time on music and 
less time laying bricks. After they left Decatur Street 
they went to Stell’s Wiggling Wagon on St. Claude and 
then played at the Club Playtime in Bunkie, La., then off 
and on for twenty years at Luthjens dance hall, at Almon- 
aster and Marais Street, fondly called the “old folks’ 
home” because elderly couples from the neighborhood 
used to come there to dance. The stints at Luthjens were 
interspersed with various jobs; they accompanied Ida Cox 
on her first southern tour and played many small French 
Quarter joints such as the Cave, the Cat and the Fiddle, 
and the Pig Pen where Alphonse Picou was the leader. 

In the 1950’s both Billie and De De were plagued 
with ill health. After De De’s eyesight failed and Billie 
spent long months in the hospital even their closest 
friends thought their musical careers were ended. But in 
the 60’s they began one of the most remarkable come- 
backs in New Orleans history. 


